# Mechanism: pinned-refs — the REFRESH registry for pins written at their point of use.
# Tool: src/Core.TypeScript/ace/refresh-pins.ts   (`ace refresh`)
#
# THIS FILE IS A MIRROR, NEVER A SOURCE. Nothing in any build path reads it.
# Every pin it records is written where it is USED — a container digest is
# `FROM image:tag@sha256:...` inside the Dockerfile, and `docker build` resolves
# it with no package manager present. Delete this file, delete all of `ace`, and
# every pinned dependency in the tree still resolves exactly as it did.
# `.claude/rules/clone-at-tag-stays-sufficient.md`: `ace` may be the good path
# and may never be the only one — the discriminator is EXIT, not degree.
#
# `bun src/Core.TypeScript/ace/refresh-pins.ts --verify` is the falsifier for
# that claim: it refuses whenever a row's digest differs from the digest actually
# written at its point of use. If this file ever quietly became the source of
# truth, the inline pin would drift and --verify would go red.
#
# ── WHY A REGISTRY AT ALL ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A pin nobody can refresh is how a security posture rots: a base image frozen in
# March keeps every CVE it shipped with, and does so silently. A refresh that does
# not re-pin is how supply-chain integrity is lost. So the two are ONE mechanism,
# and the rows below are what makes the refresh possible and the staleness
# REPORTABLE — `--report` classifies each row fresh / moved / past-horizon /
# unknown, so "we pinned it and never looked again" is a finding, not a silence.
#
# UNKNOWN IS NOT FRESH. A resolver that could not run reports `unknown`, which is
# printed as its own class and never folded into the fresh count.
#
# ── FORMAT ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   <kind>  <file>  <ref>  <pin>  update=  remeasure=  horizon=  pinned=
#
#   kind      container | npm-advisory | nuget | npm-lock | pip   (closed set)
#   file      the POINT OF USE — the committed file the pin is written into
#   ref       the mutable upstream name the pin is anchored to
#   pin       sha256:<64 hex>, or a version for npm-advisory
#   update    follow-tag (refresh re-resolves) | frozen (refresh refuses)
#   remeasure MANDATORY, colon-encoded argv. The run that must exit 0 before a new
#             pin is kept. A row without it is a PARSE ERROR, not a default — a
#             digest nothing re-measured is an unverified bump wearing a pin's
#             clothes, and it lands as a one-line diff that reads as housekeeping.
#   horizon   days after which the row is reported stale even if upstream has not
#             moved. A POLICY number, not a measurement: 60d for base images is a
#             judgement about how long an unexamined pin should be allowed to sit,
#             not a claim about anyone's rebuild cadence.
#   pinned    YYYY-MM-DD the current pin was measured
#
# ── WHAT "CHECKED" MEANS PER KIND — THEY DIFFER, AND THAT IS THE POINT ───────
#
#   container   The digest is the registry's manifest digest for the tag.
#               Re-measure = the Dockerfile still BUILDS on the new base. Weak but
#               real: it catches a base whose toolchain or layout moved under us.
#               Resolver: implemented (Registry HTTP API v2, Docker-Content-Digest).
#
#   npm-advisory  Not a file pin. The subject is a DISMISSED advisory whose premise
#               can expire, and the thing being re-checked is "does upstream still
#               publish no release that admits the patched dependency?" Re-measure
#               is by hand, because reversing a dismissal is a judgement.
#               Resolver: implemented (npm registry; latest release's declared range).
#
#   nuget       Pinned by `packages.lock.json` contentHash; checked by
#               `dotnet restore --locked-mode`. NO RESOLVER YET — a row of this kind
#               reports `unknown`, which is the honest answer.
#   npm-lock    Pinned by `package-lock.json` integrity; checked by `npm ci`. Same.
#   pip         Pinned by the PyPI artifact hash; checked by
#               `pip install --require-hashes`. Same.
#
#   downloadThenRun  HAS NO KIND HERE ON PURPOSE. A fetched file is already owned by
#               `tools/setup/manifests/from-url` (sha256 mandatory per row, the
#               realizer refuses a row without one) and refreshed by
#               `tools/setup/repin-rolling.ts`. Building a second mechanism for the
#               same subject would be the duplication this registry exists to avoid.
#
# ── PROVENANCE OF THE DIGESTS BELOW ──────────────────────────────────────────
#
# MEASURED 2026-09-09 by `refresh-pins.ts --resolve` against each registry's
